VulkanLumen — Stage 7: screen-space tracing + scene-BVH fallback

The fixed vulkan-experiment harness still calls the same Lumen API.
All ray algorithms and GI are owned by vulkan-systems/lumen/.

Current implementation
  - Rasterized original Cornell scene and ceiling area light
  - Fast one-ray BVH direct shadows
  - Stage 4 material cards (16 faces, 64x64 RGBA8 each)
  - Stage 5 world-space directional diffuse radiance probes (12x8x12)
  - Stage 6 visibility-aware screen-probe gather at 16x16 tile spacing
  - Stage 7: one deterministic cosine-biased secondary ray per screen probe
    (no screen temporal accumulation, TAA, or denoising)

Stage 7 ray path
  1. Trace 8 evenly spaced candidate points along the secondary world ray.
  2. Project each position into the current camera's depth/color textures.
  3. Accept a screen hit only when the sampled geometry is within a narrow
     linear-depth thickness, faces the ray and is not the source face.
     Screen hits reuse the visible surface's lit HDR color.
  4. On an unresolved/out-of-screen ray, trace the same ray through the
     already-resident VulkanBVH-compatible triangle buffers.
  5. A fallback BVH hit evaluates the material card and direct area light
     at the hit; misses contribute no new screen correction.
  6. Compare the single secondary sample to the world-cache irradiance.
     Clamp and blend the delta weakly into the existing screen-probe result.
     Stage 5 global illumination always remains the base fallback.

M1 budget
  - One new local secondary ray per 16x16 screen probe, not per pixel
  - Up to 8 screen-depth steps, and a BVH fallback only if unresolved
  - Max distance and correction strength are set in lumen::settings:
      screen_trace_distance = 3.0f   (clamped to 0.5..8 meters)
      screen_trace_strength = 0.12f  (clamped to 0..0.25)
  - The existing 2 BVH visibility rays/probe remain Stage 6's maximum.
  - The existing GPU 'screen' timing includes Stage 6+7 together.
  - Separate GPU cards/GI/scene/screen/composite/total measurements remain.
  - No claim of 60 FPS until the Stage 7 build is tested on the base M1.

Known limitations
  - One ray per tile is not an unbiased GI estimator and does not replace
    Screen Probe Gather's world-space cache. The weak/clamped correction
    avoids noticeable flickering but may underrepresent contact light.
  - Screen-depth hits can be missed on thin surfaces, at offscreen geometry,
    at high ray angles or near depth discontinuities; the BVH fallback
    handles unresolved rays with the existing Cornell geometry.
  - The fallback currently lights hits with direct lighting, while screen
    hits reuse the complete visible HDR radiance; this is only an approximate
    hit-lighting strategy, not a full card-lighting atlas.
  - The geometry BVH is static and CPU-built, not full GPU-built BLAS/TLAS.
  - No generic mesh loader, distance fields, dynamic scene invalidation,
    multibounce cache, dedicated reflection tracing or full Lumen cards.
  - Camera-dependent screen traces can cause view-dependent color bleeding.
  - No actual GPU validation / macOS compile has been performed here.

Run from vulkan-experiment/
  git pull --ff-only
  rm -f c.lock
  c build run vulkan-experiment

Expected: 'Stage 7', a screen-probe grid size, trace=8 steps+3m BVH fallback.
Send the compile log, screenshot, and GPU scene/screen/total timings for
the same camera view used in Stage 6.

Milestones
  1  Cornell primary visibility and composite              implemented
  2  Direct area lighting and shadows                      implemented
  3  Static generic BVH ray queries (Cornell demo)          implemented
  4  Basic material surface-card atlas                     implemented
  5  World-space directional single-bounce radiance cache  implemented
  6  Visibility-aware screen probes                        implemented
  7  Short screen ray with BVH fallback                    implemented
  8  Dynamic invalidation and multibounce transport        not implemented
  9  Dedicated rough/specular reflections                  not implemented
  10 Profiling, quality scaling and validation             partial

These are milestones for a minimal Lumen-inspired renderer, not a claim that
Epic's full Lumen system is 70% reconstructed. Full scene support, automatic
surface-card capture, robust visibility, multiple tracing backends and
dynamic data remain substantial additional work.
